const hostJson = `{
  "controlPort": 41001,
  "mediaPort": 41002,
  "discoveryPort": 41000,
  "codec": "hevc",
  "chroma": "nv12",
  "fps": 120,
  "maxBitrateKbps": 100000,
  "clipboard": "both",
  "requirePINForLAN": true,
  "allowTailscaleSameUser": true
}`;
---

<Docs slug="configuration">
	<h2 id="files">Config files</h2>
	<p>
		Both binaries read one directory: <code>planck</code> inside the operating system's user config
		directory. <code>-config &lt;dir&gt;</code> points either binary somewhere else.
	</p>
	<Table label="Config directory by platform">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Platform</th><th scope="col">Directory</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>Windows</td><td><code>%APPDATA%\planck</code></td></tr>
				<tr><td>macOS</td><td><code>~/Library/Application Support/planck</code></td></tr>
				<tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/planck</code>, or <code>~/.config/planck</code></td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<Table label="Files in the config directory">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">Holds</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td><code>host.json</code></td><td>Host settings</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>client.json</code></td><td>App settings, paired hosts, hotkeys, the telemetry switch</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>host-cert.pem</code>, <code>host-key.pem</code></td><td>The host's self-signed certificate and key</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>paired-clients.json</code></td><td>Client fingerprints this host has paired</td></tr>
				<tr><td><code>telemetry.json</code></td><td>The random install ID and whether the first launch was counted</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<p>Uninstalling on Windows keeps this directory, so a reinstall keeps its pairings.</p>

	<h2 id="host-json">Host settings</h2>
	<p>A <code>host.json</code> with the defaults written out:</p>
	<CodeBlock code={hostJson} lang="json" caption="host.json" what="host.json defaults" />
	<Table label="host.json keys">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{hostKeys.map(([k, d, n]) => <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{d}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>)}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<p>
		The host validates <code>codec</code> and <code>chroma</code> against the lists above, rejects a PIN
		that is not 6 digits, and refuses a config whose control and media ports match.
	</p>

	<h2 id="client-json">Client settings</h2>
	<p>
		The app writes <code>client.json</code> from Settings; editing it by hand works too. Missing keys take
		their defaults.
	</p>
	<Table label="client.json keys">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{clientKeys.map(([k, d, n]) => <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{d}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>)}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>

	<h2 id="host-flags">planck-host flags</h2>
	<p>Flags apply to one run. <code>-set-name</code> is the one that writes back to <code>host.json</code>.</p>
	<Table label="planck-host flags">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Flag</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{hostFlags.map(([k, d, n]) => <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{d}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>)}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<Callout kind="caveat">
		The help text of <code>-codec</code> mentions <code>auto</code>, but the default is
		<code>hevc</code>. Pass <code>-codec auto</code> to let the host choose.
	</Callout>

	<h2 id="client-flags">planck-client flags</h2>
	<Table label="planck-client flags">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Flag</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{clientFlags.map(([k, d, n]) => <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{d}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>)}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>

	<h3 id="headless">Headless mode</h3>
	<p>
		<code>-headless</code> streams without a window and prints a summary, for benchmarks and scripted
		checks. It never sends telemetry.
	</p>
	<Table label="Headless flags">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Flag</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{headlessFlags.map(([k, d, n]) => <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{d}</td><td>{n}</td></tr>)}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<CodeBlock
		code={`planck-client -headless -discover my-host -pin 123456 -duration 10s -json
planck-client -headless -connect 192.0.2.10:41001 -path-mode lan -silent-audio`}
		lang="sh"
		caption="headless examples"
		what="headless client examples"
	/>

	<h2 id="hotkeys">Hotkeys</h2>
	<p>
		In-stream chords use Ctrl+Alt so that Cmd and the Windows key reach the host. Change them in
		Settings or in <code>client.json</code> under <code>hotkeys</code>; reserved and conflicting chords are
		refused.
	</p>
	<Table label="Default hotkeys">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Action</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Scope</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{hotkeys.map(([a, k, s]) => <tr><td>{a}</td><td><span class="kbd">{k}</span></td><td>{s}</td></tr>)}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>

	<h2 id="codecs">Codecs and chroma</h2>
	<h3 id="encoders">Encoders</h3>
	<Table label="Encoder capabilities">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Encoder</th><th scope="col">Codecs Planck drives</th><th scope="col">Chroma</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>NVENC (NVIDIA, Windows)</td><td>H.264, HEVC, AV1, as the GPU reports</td><td>NV12; P010 for 10-bit; YUV444 and YUV444P10 where the GPU supports them</td></tr>
				<tr><td>AMF (AMD, Windows)</td><td>HEVC</td><td>NV12, P010</td></tr>
				<tr><td>oneVPL (Intel, Windows)</td><td>HEVC</td><td>NV12, P010, YUV444, YUV444P10</td></tr>
				<tr><td>VideoToolbox (Apple)</td><td>HEVC (Main10 for 10-bit)</td><td>NV12, P010; 4:4:4 refused</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<h3 id="decoders">Decoders</h3>
	<Table label="Decoder capabilities">
		<table>
			<thead><tr><th scope="col">Client</th><th scope="col">HEVC</th><th scope="col">AV1</th><th scope="col">H.264</th><th scope="col">HDR10 present</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>macOS (VideoToolbox)</td><td>Yes, 10-bit</td><td>Yes, 10-bit</td><td class="is-none">No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Windows (Media Foundation)</td><td>Yes, 10-bit</td><td>Yes, 10-bit</td><td class="is-none">No</td><td>No, presents 8-bit</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</Table>
	<Callout kind="warning">
		H.264 encodes on NVENC only, and no client can decode it. Choosing <code>h264</code> on either binary
		produces a stream the client cannot show. Use HEVC (the default) or AV1.
	</Callout>
	<p>
		4:4:4 needs an encoder that reports it: NVENC (HEVC, and H.264 per its probe) or oneVPL (HEVC). AMF
		and VideoToolbox stay at 4:2:0. The client's <code>-hdr10 auto</code> turns HDR10 on where both ends
		and the host desktop have HDR, which today means a macOS client.
	</p>
</Docs>
